    "created_at": "2016-04-26T18:44:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-04-26T18:44:45Z",
    "body": "CSP is a good question. From the CSP spec it seems pretty clear that the intention of `child-src` is for it to control creation of shared/dedicated/service workers when the policy is set on the Document creating the worker (and I believe that is also what chrome has implemented, at least for dedicated workers. It mostly does so for shared workers too. Not sure what we do for service workers). But the CSP spec doesn't actually specify algorithms for this directive, and given the way HTML currently invokes fetch to install workers (with the requests client being the worker, and no reference from the request to the document that created the worker) it doesn't seem like it's actually possible to formally specify this `child-src` directive and how it would decide what requests to block.\n\nMaybe all worker fetches (other than update checks for service workers) should actually use the document/worker that created them as client of the request (consistent with subresource and navigation requests). But I haven't thought through all the consequences of such a change.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
